{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"if [ -f frontier_brief.md ]; then sed -n '1,240p' frontier_brief.md; else echo 'NO_FRONTIER_BRIEF'; fi\"","aggregated_output":"# Unverified frontier brief: dc22 level 6\n\nThis is a compact index of the latest clean WIP, not solver evidence.\nReproduce every observation you rely on with the documented local API.\nDo not reread the full proposer transcript unless a named ambiguity requires it.\n\n## Verified parent budget\n\n- Exact parent boundary: level 5 at 305 actions.\n- Remaining real-action budget under the harness cap: 295 of 600.\n- This budget is verifier evidence. If it is insufficient, optimize earlier composed legs rather than searching an uncommittable suffix.\n\n## Prior proposer progress\n\n- The exhaustive tours are halfway through: 576 configurations and 14,640 verified movement steps have visited every cell in all four portal-region components, with no reward or cyan exit. This is ruling out phase-specific floor triggers cleanly; the remaining 336 configurations cover the last seven ring placements.\n- All portal-region floor triggers are now ruled out across 23,180 exact movement steps. The analogous finite gap is the 68-cell physical/rotator component: preserved probes walked it for each ring placement, but not under all six `A` phases. I’m running the same visit-every-cell tour over 19×6×2 physical configurations.\n- Both exhaustive floor-tour matrices are negative: 42,940 movement steps cover every cell of every portal and physical component under all static phases. That leaves a transport/activation sequence, not a hidden floor trigger. I’m comparing level 6’s panel symbols with level 5’s known solved assembly to identify which icon is a destination versus a control—especially the missing magenta/blue `(13,8)` endpoint.\n- The negative physical tours rule out every static floor interaction. I’m returning to the ring as the goal object itself, with a sharper question: does rotating `B` between ring moves open placements outside the fixed 19-node graph? A static placement enumeration would miss that history-dependent route. I’m inspecting and reproducing the synchronized ring-state search specifically for novel placements, not merely reward.\n- The preserved “synchronized” ring probe fixed the global phase before moving the ring; it did not interleave rotator changes between ring steps. That is exactly the kind of history-dependent transport route a static 19-placement graph misses. I’m running a controller-only BFS whose macros always return the avatar to the D-pad center, interleaving four ring moves with off-center `B` rotation, `A`, and `S`, while checking the missing phase-1 teleport from every resulting state.\n- The dynamic interleaving search is behaving cleanly. It has reached 600 full visible states and 17 placements; so far every new placement matches the known geometric graph, and the missing phase-1 teleport remains inactive. The last two north placements are still pending, so I’m continuing through the finite controller state space.\n- All 19 placements are now reached, and interleaving `B/A/S` has produced no twentieth placement or phase-1 endpoint through 1,600 full states. The queue is shrinking, so this controller subsystem is close to exhaustive; I’m letting it terminate to distinguish a hard negative from a depth cutoff.\n- The transport subsystem is now exhaustive and negative: 1,824 interleaved controller states, exactly 19 placements, no phase-1 endpoint. The strongest remaining possibility is the avatar/bridge route itself. Earlier normalized searches sampled one `A/B` context per component; I’m reopening that subsystem with exact control tests at every reachable avatar cell while freezing the ring, starting from the verified physical entry.\n- The every-cell run immediately showed why the old search exploded: 67 equivalent `A/B` outcomes were queued separately. I’m keeping full positional coverage but grouping each result by its non-avatar world delta plus the resulting visible support component. That preserves distinct carry/rotation outcomes while collapsing cells that genuinely lead to the same post-control reachability.\n- The component grouping reduced the physical frontier from hundreds of duplicate children to roughly twenty genuine states. It has already distinguished rotator-only, upper/rotator, and phased-bridge-only outcomes, with no reward yet. Each state still verifies both controls at every reachable cell, so I’m allowing the first 50-state layer to finish before pruning further.\n- The full-context avatar search has now found the selector/hub portal through a 14-action synchronized `A` sequence that the sampled planner missed. It is at 100 genuine component states with shortest paths around 17 actions; this is the first evidence that position-complete control contexts matter. I’m continuing through the teleport layer.\n- The physical-root run hit the local execution window at 200 states, not a search terminal. It preserved a verified 14-action path into the hub/portal component. I’m restarting from that exact frontier so the next window is spent entirely on selector destinations and position-dependent `A/B` interactions, with the ring still frozen.\n\n## Preserved local probes\n\n- `capture_l6_frames.py` (1325 bytes)\n- `capture_l6_ring_montage.py` (2345 bytes)\n- `capture_level5_initial.py` (871 bytes)\n- `probe_goal_semantics.py` (2094 bytes)\n- `probe_l6.py` (1875 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_aboard_bfs.py` (2157 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_aboard_dpad.py` (2824 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_all_docks.py` (2144 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_all_ring_then_physical.py` (3722 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_bfs.py` (2059 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_bridge_avatar.py` (3965 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_candidate.py` (3614 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_canonical_macro_bfs.py` (4434 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cardinal_exits.py` (1019 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cargo_all_positions.py` (1045 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cargo_bfs.py